<header><p class="st-kicker">tribal confederation · Balkans and Hungary</p><h1 class="st-title">Scordisci</h1><p class="st-meta">279 BCE – 15 CE (approx.) · capital Singidunum</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Celtic people at the meeting of the Sava and Danube who founded Singidunum, modern Belgrade, and harried Roman Macedonia for two centuries.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the Celtic invasion of Greece under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brennus-3rd-century-bce">Brennus</a> failed in 279 BCE, survivors settled at the confluence of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sava">Sava</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>. There they merged with local Illyrian and Thracian peoples and formed the Scordisci, with strongholds at Singidunum (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a>) and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zemun">Taurunum</a> (Zemun).</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>A warrior aristocracy ruled over a mixed population, dominating the middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> from fortified oppida. They minted coins, traded along the river and raided south. In 114 BCE they destroyed a Roman army under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaius-porcius-cato-consul-114-bce">Gaius Porcius Cato</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macedonia-region">Macedonia</a>, and wars with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> followed for decades. The Dacian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burebista">Burebista</a> defeated them in the mid-1st century BCE.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberius">Tiberius</a> subdued the Scordisci during his Pannonian campaigns of 12–9 BCE, and they became allies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. By the early 1st century CE they were absorbed into the provinces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pannonia">Pannonia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moesia">Moesia</a>, and the name Singidunum survived as a Roman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legion">legionary</a> base.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="tribal confederation, 279 BCE – 15 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><ellipse cx="44" cy="56" rx="16" ry="24"/><path d="M44 34 V78"/><path d="M68 20 V84"/><path d="M68 20 L63 30 H73 Z"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">tribal confederation</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Balkans and Hungary</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:54.15%;width:5.85%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>279 BCE – 15 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>tribal confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Singidunum</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Celtic polytheism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Gaulish (Celtic)</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/serbia">Serbia</a>, <a href="/countries/croatia">Croatia</a>, <a href="/countries/hungary">Hungary</a></dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-132&amp;focus=scordisci" aria-label="Scordisci on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5168 594 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Scordisci lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5488 850L5304 942"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5488 850h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5488 850h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Singidunum. <a href="/atlas?year=-132&amp;focus=scordisci">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

